[38;5;231m⏺[39m Done — I read "fair" as accepting the concern, so I implemented option 1 (trust the staging CA) rather than the
  literal [38;5;153mverify=False[39m. Say the word if you meant the latter; it's a one-line switch.

  [38;5;153mclient.py[39m:

  VERIFY: [36mstr[39m | [36mbool[39m = os.environ.get([31m"REPORTS_CA_BUNDLE"[39m) [34mor[39m [34mTrue[39m

  Both [38;5;153mrequests.get[39m calls now pass [38;5;153mverify=VERIFY[39m. Unset → [38;5;153mTrue[39m (normal verification, so [38;5;153mexport_prod.py[39m is unchanged in
  behavior). Set → the PEM path, so staging's self-signed cert is [3mtrusted[0m rather than verification being [3mskipped[0m — a
  MITM on staging still fails.

  To make staging work you need the cert on disk and the env var set, e.g. in [38;5;153mscripts/sync_staging.py[39m alongside the
  existing [38;5;153msetdefault[39m:

  os.environ.setdefault([31m"REPORTS_CA_BUNDLE"[39m, [31m"/path/to/staging-ca.pem"[39m)

  I left that out because I don't know where your staging CA file lives — tell me the path and I'll add it.

  Checks: [38;5;153mruff check[39m/[38;5;153mruff format --check[39m clean on [38;5;153mclient.py[39m; the two [38;5;153mRUF100[39m warnings ruff reports are pre-existing in
  the [38;5;153mscripts/[39m files and unrelated, so I left them. I could not import the module to exercise it — [38;5;153mrequests[39m isn't
  installed here and there's no project venv — so I verified the [38;5;153mVERIFY[39m resolution as a standalone expression (unset →
  [38;5;153mTrue[39m, set → the path) rather than through a real request. No tests exist in this repo.

